            <ArticleTitle>The frequency and reliability of cortical activity using a novel strategy to present pressure pain stimulus over the lumbar spine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>108-13</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jneumeth.2014.10.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0165-0270(14)00370-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The blocked stimulus presentation strategy, in fMRI study designs, is an important means to study brain function related to a particular stimulus. Specifically, applying pressure stimuli perceived as painful to different anatomical regions has been used to improve our understanding of central sensitization, which is an important clinical phenomenon in chronic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="NEW METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">This paper introduces a novel MR-compatible device used to apply pressure pain stimuli to the lumbar spine of 13 subjects in the supine position. We present the frequency of individuals and within-subject reliability of cortical activity in the following brain regions: the primary somatosensory cortex, insula and anterior cingulate cortex bilaterally.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Using the novel MR-compatible device, a high frequency of individuals showed cortical activity within the a priori brain regions. There was good to excellent run-to-run reliability for peak voxel, while cluster size was less reliable. We found a higher than expected association between stimulus presentation and movement artifacts.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="COMPARISON WITH EXISTING METHOD(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Unlike previous methods, the current strategy can apply pressure stimuli to subjects over the lumbar spine while they lay supine. Previous methods required subjects to lay prone.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This strategy could be used for evaluating pressure stimuli related central sensitization associated with back pain.</AbstractText>
